I now have the complete picture. Here is the full ordered chain for POST /api/owners.

## 1. The endpoint — @RestController method

POST /api/owners is handled by `addOwner` in **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`**.

The class (lines 51-54) is annotated `@RestController`, `@CrossOrigin(exposedHeaders = "errors, content-type")`, `@RequestMapping("/api")` and implements the generated `OwnersApi` interface. Each method carries `@PreAuthorize("hasRole(@roles.OWNER_ADMIN)")`.

The handler (lines 99-109):
```java
@PreAuthorize("hasRole(@roles.OWNER_ADMIN)")
@Override
public ResponseEntity<OwnerDto> addOwner(OwnerFieldsDto ownerFieldsDto) {
    HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
    Owner owner = ownerMapper.toOwner(ownerFieldsDto);
    this.clinicService.saveOwner(owner);
    OwnerDto ownerDto = ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner);
    headers.setLocation(UriComponentsBuilder.newInstance()
        .path("/api/owners/{id}").buildAndExpand(owner.getId()).toUri());
    return new ResponseEntity<>(ownerDto, headers, HttpStatus.CREATED);
}
```

The actual HTTP mapping and parameter validation annotations live on the inherited default method in the OpenAPI-generated interface **`/home/daniel/sandbox/target/generated-sources/openapi/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/api/OwnersApi.java`** (lines 79-87):
```java
@RequestMapping(method = RequestMethod.POST, value = OwnersApi.PATH_ADD_OWNER, // "/owners"
    produces = { "application/json" }, consumes = { "application/json" })
default ResponseEntity<OwnerDto> addOwner(
    @Parameter(...required = true) @Valid @RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto ownerFieldsDto
)
```
So the body parameter is `@Valid @RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto` (full path `/api/owners` = class `/api` + `/owners`). The interface is annotated `@Validated` (line 44).

